   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the workers' compensation law, in relation to providing\nlanguage access services",
    "basePrintNo": "S6096",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6096-2023",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §17-a, Work Comp L",
    "lawSection": "Workers' Compensation Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S6096\n\nSPONSOR: RAMOS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the workers' compensation law, in relation to providing\nlanguage access services\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to amend the Worker's Compensation Law in\nrelation to language access services to ensure consistency with the\nprovisions under the Executive Law.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one of the bill amends Section 17-a of the Worker's Compensation\nLaw to align provisions requiring document translation and language\naccess services with the requirements under Section 202-a of the Execu-\ntive Law.\n\nSection two of the bill establishes the effective date.\n\n \nDIFFERENCE BETWEEN ORIGINAL AND AMENDED VERSION (IF APPLICABLE):\n\nN/A\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nNew York State is proudly diverse, with more than two hundred languages\nspoken by its residents. Half of all New York City residents speak a\nlanguage other than English at home, and 13% of New York State residents\nhave limited English proficiency (LEP.) The linguistic diversity of New\nYork State demands that accommodations be made for those who have\nlanguage access needs. Many New Yorkers require translation of key docu-\nments and interpretation of various proceedings when interacting with\ngovernment agencies and programs.\n\nAddressing language access needs is crucial in the Workers' Compensation\ncontext.  LEP workers often work more dangerous, physically demanding,\nlower paying jobs than workers who speak fluent English. For example, in\n2014, 32.3% of people working construction jobs in the United States\nwere Latino or Hispanic, while Latino and Hispanic individuals make up\nonly 16.1% of people working in all occupations. That same year,\nconstruction-related jobs were among the most dangerous in the country.\nAccording to OSHA, 903 Latino workers were killed on the job in 2015.\nWhen these workers are injured, they require translation and interpreta-\ntion services in order to receive the Workers' Compensation benefits to\nwhich they are entitled.  Competent interpretation services are crucial\nin Workers' Compensation proceedings. Workers' Compensation is a compli-\ncated area of law, and hearings employ a high amount of technical legal\nlanguage and acronyms. WCB proceedings can be challenging for any\nlayperson to understand, and lack of language access further complicate\nclaimants' ability to understand proceedings that are essential to their\nhealth and livelihood.\n\nAccordingly, it is crucial that New York's language access standards are\nconsistent across its various agencies. Aligning the language access\nstandards of the worker's compensation law with those already in the\nexecutive law will remove confusion as to what is required of New York\nState agencies so that those in need of language access services in the\nworkers' compensation system can be assured they will receive those\nservices as needed.\n\nThis bill would ensure that the language access plan published by the\nWorkers' Compensation Board is consistent with those implemented by\nother state agencies to provide crucial services to claimants.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew Bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nNone to the State.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
